<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.10.3: Comparing Functions Represented in Separate Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01027</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.10.3: Comparing Functions Represented in Separate Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>92e8dfd0-6db9-43e8-9a0b-7395bb5f18ae</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>The number of people who watched a TV episode is a function of that show’s episode number. Here are three graphs of three functions—\(A\), \(B\), and \(C\)—representing three different TV shows.</para>
      <div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-align-items-center">
        <para>Show A (<media alt="10 data points on coordinate plane."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_06_01.svg"/></media>)</para>
        <para>Show B (<media alt="A graph. "><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_06_02.svg"/></media>)</para>
        <para>Show C (<media alt="10 data points on coordinate plane."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_06_03.svg"/></media>)</para>
      
      <para>Match each description with a graph that could represent the situation described. One of the descriptions has no corresponding graph.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_55e82363-cfbc-47ef-9686-e02aaf346a67" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>This show has a good core audience. It had a guest star in the fifth episode that brought in some new viewers, but most of them stopped watching after that.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph of function \(C\)</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: graph of function \(C\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3d080c5a-8054-479a-9f61-4bd9fa4b3120" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>This show is one of the most popular shows, and its audience keeps increasing.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! no match</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: no match</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="bfa3a562-185d-4ba8-b510-49dbc5bb5223" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>This show has a small audience, but it’s improving, so more people are noticing.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph of function \(A\)</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: graph of function \(A\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_3c5faec7-0e76-4ed2-a378-ea37bb6269a1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>This show started out huge. Even though it feels like it crashed, it still has more viewers than another show.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph of function \(B\)</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: graph of function \(B\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_074f8390-8547-4d20-a6b1-175f0c152fbf" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which is greatest, \(A(7)\), \(B(7)\), \(C(7)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(B(7)\)</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is: \(B(7)\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Explain what the answer to question 5 tells us about the shows.</para>
            
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
    3.6 million people watched the 7th episode of Show B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course to sketch a graph of the viewership of the fourth TV show that did not have a matching graph. 
 </item>
      </list>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a59acb00a5280744c0480cc59ede55347c17f6f3.html" width="500" height="400"/><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>When you have finished graphing, select the Solution button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Discrete function. episode number and audience in millions.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_06_04.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <title>Video: Comparing Functions Represented in Separate Graphs</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about how to compare functions that are represented in separate graphs.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7529123fb47025e11d6485c60baf00b6394e707b"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check">
          <para>For which of the following functions is \(f(3)\) the greatest value?</para>
        </note>
        <section>
          <title>Additional Resources</title>
          
    <section>
      <title> Comparing Real-Life Functions</title>
      <para>Two people, Adam and Bianca, are competing to see who can save more money in one month. Use the table and the graph below to determine who will save more money at the end of the month. State how much money each person had at the start of the competition. (Assume each is following a linear function in his or her saving habit.) Let Adam’s function be represented by \(A(t)\), where \(t\) is days, and Bianca’s function be represented by \(B(t)\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="TWO FUNCTIONS REPRESENTING SAVINGS IN DOLLARS AS A FUNCTION OF NUMBER OF DAYS FOR TWO PEOPLE: ADAM AND BIANCA. THE TOTAL AMOUNT ADAM HAS SAVED IS REPRESENTED BY THE GRAPH OF A LINE THAT PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (0, 3) AND (2, 9). THE TOTAL AMOUNT BIANCA HAS SAVED IS REPRESENTED BY THE GRAPH OF A LINE THAT PASSES THROUGH THE POINTS (0, 2) AND (2, 8). ">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_08_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>Which is greater, \(A(2)\) or \(B(2)\)? Explain what this answer means.</para>
      <para>\(A(2)=9\) and \(B(2)=8\), so after 2 days, Adam has saved more money.</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Comparing Real-Life Functions</title>
      <para>Aleph is running at a constant rate on a flat, paved road. The graph below, \(A(t)\), represents the total distance, \(A\), he covers with respect to time, \(t\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH THAT SHOWS TOTAL DISTANCE IN MILES AS A FUNCTION OF TIME IN MINUTES. THE GRAPH IS A STRAIGHT LINE THAT INCREASES FROM LEFT TO RIGHT AND PASSES THROUGH THE POINT (40, 5.5).&#10;">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_08_03.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>Shannon is running on a flat, rocky trail that eventually rises up a steep mountain. The graph below, \(S(t)\), represents the total distance, \(S\), she covers with respect to time, \(t\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH THAT SHOWS TOTAL DISTANCE IN MILES AS A FUNCTION OF TIME IN MINUTES. THE GRAPH IS NONLINEAR AND INCREASES FROM LEFT TO RIGHT AND INCLUDES THE POINT (40, 2.5).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_08_04.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Who has traveled farther after 40 minutes? Explain your answer.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
   </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to determine who went a greater distance:</para>
            <para>Aleph traveled farther in 40 minutes. Comparing the graphs, \(A(40)\) is 5.5 miles, and \(S(40)\) is approximately 2.5 miles.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
        </section>
        
